<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mont-Saint-Michel</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mont-Saint-Michel is a tidal island off the coast of Normandy, near the border with Brittany, crowned by a Benedictine abbey dedicated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkhangelsk">Archangel</a> Michael. A sanctuary was founded there in 708 and a Benedictine community in 966 under the dukes of Normandy. Its fortified abbey resisted English sieges during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>, and the site is now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mont-saint-michel.jpg" alt="Mont-Saint-Michel" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Amaustan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mont-Saint-Michel_vu_du_ciel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
